# .gitignore
# ============================================================================
# File:     otsafety-gnn/.gitignore
# Purpose:  Root-level Git ignore rules preventing tool artifacts, secrets,
#           and platform-specific build outputs from ever being tracked in
#           this repository, copied from FleetManagement.
#
# Why it exists:
#   The Frozen Stack (Fleet-Management-Stack.pdf) mandates a polyglot
#   monorepo with pnpm workspaces + Turborepo, Expo driver-app, Next.js
#   ops-web, NestJS api, BullMQ worker, and Terraform IaC. Each of those
#   tools produces build artifacts, caches, or state files that must not
#   enter version control — and critically, secret material (.env*,
#   Terraform state, signing keys) must be blocked at the source. This
#   file is the first line of defense; the pre-commit hooks in
#   .pre-commit-config.yaml are the second.
#
# Key decisions / invariants:
#   - .env* is blocked globally with allowlist exceptions for .env.example
#     (template files that MUST be committed so new contributors can scaffold
#     their local env) and .env.sops.yaml (SOPS/age ciphertext — see below).
#   - Terraform state (*.tfstate, *.tfvars) is blocked because it contains
#     plaintext DB passwords, S3 credentials, and IdP secrets. Only
#     *.tfvars.example is allowed.
#   - Expo native build directories (apps/driver-app/ios, android) and
#     signing materials (*.jks, *.p8, *.p12, *.mobileprovision) are blocked
#     — these are regenerated by EAS Build and must never be committed.
#   - .turbo/ is the local Turborepo cache; always machine-specific.
#   - .pnpm-store/ is pnpm's content-addressed store.
#   - .vscode/* blocked with explicit allowlist for settings.json and
#     extensions.json (shared team editor setup).
#   - Keeps !negation patterns close to their block patterns for readability.
#
# Related files:
#   - .pre-commit-config.yaml  — hooks that re-enforce these bans at commit
#   - SECURITY.md              — rationale for secret-blocking policy
#   - .github/workflows/ci.yml — CI uses pnpm cache paths these rules cover
# ============================================================================

# Node / pnpm
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/
.pnpm-debug.log*
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Build outputs
dist/
build/
out/
.next/
.turbo/
.expo/
.expo-shared/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Env / secrets
#
# .env.sops.yaml is TRACKED ON PURPOSE and is the one exception that carries
# real credential material. It is SOPS/age ciphertext: every VALUE is encrypted
# to the public keys in .age-recipients, while field NAMES stay plaintext so the
# file reviews and diffs like ordinary config. Committing it is what makes a new
# machine reproducible -- the alternative, which this replaces, was copying a
# plaintext .env between laptops by hand, leaving secrets on N machines with no
# rotation path and no record of which host held which vintage.
#
# The allowlist is exact (.env.sops.yaml), never a glob such as .env.*.yaml: a
# pattern would silently admit any future file whose name happens to match,
# which is precisely how plaintext gets committed by accident.
#
# .env itself stays blocked. Produce it with: pnpm run env:decrypt
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sops.yaml
*.pem
*.key

# age PRIVATE identities. The public half lives in .age-recipients (tracked);
# the private half must never leave the machine that generated it. Blocked here
# as defense in depth even though the conventional location is outside the repo
# (~/.config/sops/age/keys.txt, mode 600).
*.agekey
age-key.txt
keys.txt

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json

# Logs / coverage / test artifacts
logs/
*.log
coverage/
.nyc_output/
junit.xml
playwright-report/
test-results/

# Terraform
infra/terraform/.terraform/
infra/terraform/*.tfstate
infra/terraform/*.tfstate.*
infra/terraform/*.tfvars
!infra/terraform/*.tfvars.example
infra/terraform/.terraform.lock.hcl.backup

# Expo / native
apps/driver-app/ios/
apps/driver-app/android/
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.mobileprovision

# Misc
*.tgz
.cache/
packages/*/reports/
packages/*/.stryker-tmp/
/playwright/.cache/

# Stryker mutation-testing temporary artifacts (large; never commit)
.stryker-tmp/
**/.stryker-tmp/

# Stryker mutation testing artifacts
reports/

# curl debugging artifacts
cookies.txt

# E2E runtime handoff artifacts (driver-leg credentials emitted by dispatcher-to-driver spec)
.e2e-artifacts/

# TS incremental + Vite optimizer caches (stale-replay hygiene)
.vite/

# ============================================================================
# --- Python / uv / ML artifacts ---
# Why it exists: this file was copied from FleetManagement, whose
# pnpm-workspace.yaml banner states the mandate as "pnpm workspaces +
# Turborepo; Node/TS only". It therefore carries no Python rules at all.
# This repo is polyglot: python/otsafety is the GNN package.
#
# Key decision: added BEFORE the root commit, not after. `uv sync` creates
# .venv and pytest creates .pytest_cache on first run; an untracked-but-
# unignored .venv gets swept up by `git add -A` and removing it from history
# costs far more than these lines.
# ============================================================================
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv/
venv/
# .python-version is TRACKED: it is the CPython pin flake.nix reads and
# uv installs from, so every environment must receive it.
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
# Run artifacts: evidence is generated, never committed as source.
mlruns/
artifacts/
outputs/
*.parquet
*.pkl
*.pth
*.onnx

# --- Shared evidence and scratch ---
# Every worktree records into the MAIN checkout's .artifacts/, because
# `git worktree remove` deletes ignored files along with the directory.
# .cache/ holds PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX bytecode; .idea/ is IntelliJ's, kept out so
# an IDE cannot stage its own settings.
.artifacts/
.cache/
.idea/

# --- The project site ---
# Build output and generated types; bun.lock is TRACKED, because without it
# --frozen-lockfile has nothing to be frozen against.
apps/site/node_modules/
apps/site/dist/
apps/site/.astro/

# --- Generated documents ---
# The one-page sheet is generated from apps/site/src/data, never authored, so
# committing it would create a second copy to keep in step. mise run pdf:build
# rebuilds it, and CI uploads it as a workflow artifact.
build/
